                <pb facs="1333" n="1209" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg1209"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.602 Ot 81/300</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Geddes</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-05-01"><hi rendition="#italic">May 1,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: I have received your
                    letter of April 30th in which, in referring to statements noted in the Press
                    that the concession recently granted to Admiral Chester by the Angora Government
                    comprises certain rights in Mesopotamia, you indicate the view of your
                    Government with regard to such a contingency.</p>
                <p>While the Department has not, as yet, received a final text of the concession in
                    question, I take this occasion to assure you that careful consideration will be
                    given to the observations which you have presented when more definite
                    information as to the exact nature of the grant is available.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
